= El cerco en San Vicente

They have put a cordon around a coffee plantation, which is a thing that sounds workable on a map and is not.

Forty square kilometres of shade trees with nine metres of visibility in it, and three hundred pickers inside the cordon along with you, and a commander who now has to decide whether he is going to search it row by row — which would take his battalion eleven days — or squeeze it, or burn it.

He is going to squeeze it. They always squeeze. Which means the ring gets smaller every day and at some point there is not enough coffee left to be inside.

- @cer_sv_cortadores: **Salir con los cortadores.** The pickers leave every evening at six, three hundred of them, in a column, carrying sacks. *(walk out through the cordon in plain sight; they are searched)*
- @cer_sv_acequia: **La acequia.** The irrigation channel that feeds the beneficio runs out under the road. *(concrete, waist-deep, and it goes exactly one place)*
- @foco_batalla.cer_debil: **Salir por lo débil.** Find the thin sector of the ring and go through the rows.
- @foco_batalla.cer_disolverse: **Disolverse.** Into the coffee, in ones and twos, for a month.

@cer_sv_cortadores
