= El cerco en El Porvenir

Whoever drew this ring walked every metre of it, which tells you these are not conscripts and this is not a sweep.

Two thousand metres, no road within eleven kilometres, and a cordon of men on foot holding four cols and a ridge. It is a remarkable piece of work and it has one flaw in it that they cannot fix: at four o'clock, as it has every afternoon since the Pleistocene, the cloud comes up the valley, and for seventeen hours nobody up here can see anything at all.

They know this. There is nothing they can do about it. You can watch them thinking about it.

- @cer_ep_niebla: **A las cuatro.** Walk out through the cordon inside the cloud. *(the mountain's own gift; total blindness, theirs and yours)*
- @cer_ep_esperar: **Que se les acabe.** Do not go anywhere. They are carrying eleven days of food and you are being fed by four hamlets. Outlast the ring. *(the ring dissolves itself; it takes a fortnight of nerve)*
- @foco_batalla.cer_fuerte: **Salir por lo fuerte.** Break the cordon at its strongest col.
- @foco_batalla.cer_disolverse: **Disolverse.** Ones and twos into the high country.

@cer_ep_niebla
go-to: foco_batalla.cer_fin
